            0.595,
            0.072,
            0.814,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "Two Variables, One Line"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.851,
            0.071,
            0.882,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "89"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.248,
            0.119,
            0.751,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.58,
            0.885,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": "We have already seen in the chapter on Linear Polynomials that plotting the solutions of an equation of the form y = mx + d leads to a straight line joining them. Every solution (x, y) of the equation is a point on the line."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.661,
            0.887,
            0.752
          ],
          "content": "Clearly the solutions of $y = - \\frac{3}{2}x + 6$ are the same as those of $3x + 2y = 12$ . Hence the graph of 3x + 2y = 12 is the same as that of $y = - \\frac{3}{2}x + 6$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.755,
            0.884,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": "**Example 2:** Find any two solutions of 2x + y = 7. Draw the graph of this equation. Use the graph to find 4 more solutions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.798,
            0.885,
            0.873
          ],
          "content": "When we substitute x = 0 in the equation, we get $(2 \\times 0) + y = 7,$ that is, y = 7. Hence one solution is (0, 7). By substituting y = 0, we get $2x + 0 = 7$ , that is, $x = { \\frac { 7 } { 2 } }$ . Thus, $\\left( { \\frac { 7 } { 2 } } , 0 \\right)$ is also a solution."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
